{"Priority-based provider selection is capability-agnostic - a high-priority provider can be selected even if it lacks required operations, causing runtime failures from partial implementations": 0.47932466864585876, "Consider adding capability validation at initialization time or implementing feature-based provider resolution to prevent runtime failures": 0.11161687970161438, "Initializing CryptoIntegration as a side effect of create() introduces global/static crypto setup that may not be expected by library consumers, creating classloader coupling or initialization-order issues in embedded runtimes": 0.4500831961631775, "The side effect of CryptoIntegration initialization should be documented in the method's Javadoc, or an explicit initialization method separate from client creation should be provided": 0.2549712061882019, "Minimal provider implements a broad interface with 15+ methods throwing UnsupportedOperationException, violating the Liskov Substitution Principle": 0.3424001634120941, "Consider splitting the SPI into smaller capability-specific interfaces to address the partial implementation problem": 0.009487684816122055, "Consider adding runtime guards/tests to ensure only supported methods are reachable in the minimal provider": 0.00572288129478693, "Consider implementing capability discovery in the provider selection logic": 0.006827730219811201, "Provider precedence is controlled by numeric ordering constants across different modules without any mechanism to enforce or validate the contract": 0.15207234025001526, "Silent provider ignoring (debug-log only) hides potential misconfiguration in production deployments": 0.40755102038383484, "CryptoProvider interface semantics shifted from single complete provider to possibly partial prioritized provider but the interface and documentation don't communicate this change": 0.3328089118003845}